@@ -1,35 +1,35 @@
 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.conosur.05.en-tanto-que",
-  "title": "En tanto que: matizar una comparación entre dos casos",
+  "title": "A pesar de: in spite of the obstacle",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "*En tanto que* has two related uses: as a formal connector meaning whereas/while, contrasting two parallel cases (similar to *mientras que* but more formal), and as a phrase meaning insofar as/in one's capacity as, framing something in terms of a specific role or category. Both appear in historical comparison writing. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "content": "You know *a pesar de* + noun or infinitive, *a pesar de que* + a verb, and *pese a* from B1 Unit 32. They introduce an obstacle that did not stop what happened next. Transitions to democracy are full of such obstacles: laws that protected the military, immunity, referendums."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "Argentina optó por juicios penales tempranos, en tanto que Chile prefirió, al menos inicialmente, un modelo basado en comisiones de la verdad.",
-          "english": "Argentina opted for early criminal trials, whereas Chile preferred, at least initially, a model based on truth commissions."
+          "spanish": "A pesar de esas leyes, muchas organizaciones siguieron pidiendo justicia.",
+          "english": "In spite of those laws, many organisations kept asking for justice."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Uruguay, en tanto que caso singular dentro de la región, vivió un patrón distinto.",
-          "english": "Uruguay, as a singular case within the region, experienced a different pattern."
+          "spanish": "A pesar de su inmunidad como senador vitalicio, Pinochet fue detenido en Londres.",
+          "english": "In spite of his immunity as a senator for life, Pinochet was arrested in London."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Estos tres caminos, en tanto que ejemplos comparables dentro de una misma región, muestran la ausencia de una única fórmula.",
-          "english": "These three paths, as comparable examples within the same region, show the absence of a single formula."
+          "spanish": "La ley siguió vigente a pesar de que muchas personas la criticaban.",
+          "english": "The law stayed in force although many people criticised it."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "La Comisión Valech, en tanto que segunda comisión de la verdad, amplió considerablemente el registro oficial de víctimas.",
-          "english": "The Valech Commission, as a second truth commission, considerably expanded the official record of victims."
+          "spanish": "Pese a los cambios de gobierno, las familias siguieron pidiendo verdad.",
+          "english": "Despite the changes of government, the families kept asking for the truth."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "When *en tanto que* contrasts two clauses (whereas), it behaves like *mientras que*. When it follows a noun directly (*en tanto que caso singular*), it works like *en calidad de* -- pay attention to what follows it to know which meaning is in play."
+      "content": "Never drop *de*: *a pesar de*, not *a pesar* alone. Use *a pesar de* before a noun or infinitive (*a pesar de esas leyes*) and *a pesar de que* before a conjugated verb (*a pesar de que la criticaban*). *Pese a* means the same and is a little more formal."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-represionpolitica-01-en-virtud-de-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-represionpolitica-01-en-virtud-de-gr.json
index af86911d..5a64dc3e 100644
--- a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-represionpolitica-01-en-virtud-de-gr.json
+++ b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-represionpolitica-01-en-virtud-de-gr.json
@@ -1,35 +1,35 @@
 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.represionpolitica.01.en-virtud-de",
-  "title": "En virtud de: la base legal de una acción",
+  "title": "The passive with ser: who was watched, who was detained",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "*En virtud de* (by virtue of, under) introduces the formal legal or official basis for an action -- common in historical writing about laws, decrees, and institutions. It's followed by a noun phrase, and often carries a slightly skeptical edge when the \"legal basis\" cited is itself questionable. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "content": "You know the passive with *ser* + past participle from B1 Unit 18. The person or thing that receives the action is the subject, and the participle agrees with it in gender and number: *vigilado*, *vigilada*, *vigilados*, *vigiladas*. If you name the doer, it comes after *por*. Writing about repression uses the passive to put the victims first."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "El estado de sitio suspendía derechos constitucionales básicos, en virtud de una supuesta emergencia nacional.",
-          "english": "The state of siege suspended basic constitutional rights, by virtue of a supposed national emergency."
+          "spanish": "Los opositores eran vigilados por la policía.",
+          "english": "The opponents were watched by the police."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "En virtud de estos decretos, las fuerzas de seguridad podían detener a cualquier persona sin necesidad de una orden judicial.",
-          "english": "By virtue of these decrees, security forces could detain any person without needing a judicial order."
+          "spanish": "Las garantías de los ciudadanos eran suspendidas.",
+          "english": "Citizens' guarantees were suspended."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "En virtud de su supuesta legalidad, el régimen presentaba estas medidas como una simple respuesta a una amenaza real.",
-          "english": "By virtue of their supposed legality, the regime presented these measures as a simple response to a real threat."
+          "spanish": "Muchas personas eran detenidas de noche.",
+          "english": "Many people were arrested at night."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "En virtud del control que ejercía sobre el poder judicial, el régimen se aseguraba de que ningún tribunal cuestionara estas prácticas.",
-          "english": "By virtue of the control it exercised over the judiciary, the regime made sure no court would question these practices."
+          "spanish": "Los prisioneros eran torturados en centros clandestinos.",
+          "english": "The prisoners were tortured in clandestine centres."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "*En virtud de* is more formal than por or debido a -- reach for it specifically when the reading needs to name the legal or official justification behind an action, not just any general cause."
+      "content": "Use *eran* + participle for something that happened again and again, and *fue* or *fueron* + participle for one event: *El estado de sitio fue declarado en 1973.* The participle always agrees with the subject: *las garantías eran suspendidas*, not *suspendido*."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-represionpolitica-02-con-el-fin-de-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-represionpolitica-02-con-el-fin-de-gr.json
