   "id": "grammar.b1.represionpolitica.02.con-el-fin-de",
-  "title": "Con el fin de: expresar un propósito",
+  "title": "Se with a verb: books banned, teachers dismissed",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "*Con el fin de* (with the aim of, in order to) introduces the purpose behind an action -- a more formal alternative to para, common in historical prose describing policy or institutional goals. It's followed by an infinitive when the subject of both clauses is the same. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "content": "You know *se* with a verb from B1 Unit 6. It lets you say what was done without saying who did it. With a noun, the verb agrees with it (*se prohibían libros*). With no noun, the verb stays singular (*se prohibía hablar*). Here is a case that mixes both: people. They take the personal *a* (you know it from A1 Unit 24), and the verb stays singular: *se vigilaba a los periodistas*. This suits a topic where the doers are the state, the police or the army and you want to focus on what happened."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "Con el fin de controlar la información que llegaba a la población, estos regímenes impusieron censores oficiales en los principales medios.",
-          "english": "With the aim of controlling the information reaching the population, these regimes imposed official censors on the main media outlets."
+          "spanish": "Se vigilaba a los periodistas.",
+          "english": "The journalists were watched."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Muchos libros fueron prohibidos y quemados, con el fin de eliminar cualquier material considerado subversivo.",
-          "english": "Many books were banned and burned, with the aim of eliminating any material considered subversive."
+          "spanish": "Se prohibían libros, películas y canciones.",
+          "english": "Books, films and songs were banned."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Ciertos programas académicos quedaron bajo vigilancia militar, con el fin de impedir cualquier organización política dentro de las universidades.",
-          "english": "Certain academic programs were placed under military surveillance, with the aim of preventing any political organization within the universities."
+          "spanish": "Se despidió a muchos profesores.",
+          "english": "Many teachers were dismissed."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Muchos exiliados continuaron denunciando la situación de sus países, con el fin de mantener viva la atención internacional.",
-          "english": "Many exiles kept denouncing the situation in their countries, with the aim of keeping international attention alive."
+          "spanish": "Se prohibía hablar de política.",
+          "english": "Talking about politics was forbidden."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "*Con el fin de* + infinitive requires the same subject throughout; if the subject changes, switch to *con el fin de* que + subjunctive instead, just like para vs. para que."
+      "content": "Compare *Los periodistas eran vigilados por la policía* (passive with *ser*, from Lesson 1) with *Se vigilaba a los periodistas*: the second leaves the doer out completely. Check the noun: things make the verb plural (*se quemaron libros*), people with *a* do not (*se despidió a muchos profesores*)."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-represionpolitica-03-aun-cuando-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-represionpolitica-03-aun-cuando-gr.json
index de72505a..845fb4e9 100644
--- a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-represionpolitica-03-aun-cuando-gr.json
+++ b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-represionpolitica-03-aun-cuando-gr.json
@@ -1,35 +1,35 @@
 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.represionpolitica.03.aun-cuando",
-  "title": "Aun cuando: una concesión más fuerte que aunque",
+  "title": "Según and reported statements: who gives each figure",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "*Aun cuando* (even when, even though) is a stronger, more formal concession than aunque -- useful for emphasizing that something happened despite truly extreme or unexpected circumstances. It can take the indicative for a real, known situation or the subjunctive for a hypothetical one. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "content": "You know *según* + a source and reported statements from B1 Unit 13: *según la comisión…*, *la comisión dice que…*. When a number is disputed, you must say who gives it. Each source gets its own *según*, and what people said or claimed in the past is reported with *decían que*, *afirmó que* and similar verbs."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "El gobierno negaba cualquier conocimiento de su paradero, aun cuando la familia había presenciado el propio arresto.",
-          "english": "The government denied any knowledge of their whereabouts, even when the family had witnessed the arrest itself."
+          "spanish": "Según la CONADEP, 8.961 personas desaparecieron en Argentina.",
+          "english": "According to the CONADEP, 8,961 people disappeared in Argentina."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Las Madres de Plaza de Mayo siguieron marchando, aun cuando el régimen intentó reprimir sus protestas.",
-          "english": "The Mothers of Plaza de Mayo kept marching, even when the regime tried to suppress their protests."
+          "spanish": "Según las organizaciones de derechos humanos, fueron unas 30.000.",
+          "english": "According to human-rights organisations, it was about 30,000."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Aun cuando hizo desaparecer a algunas de sus propias fundadoras, el movimiento no dejó de exigir respuestas.",
-          "english": "Even when it made some of its own founders disappear, the movement never stopped demanding answers."
+          "spanish": "Las autoridades decían que no sabían nada de las personas detenidas.",
+          "english": "The authorities said they knew nothing about the people detained."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Muchísimos casos permanecen sin resolver, aun cuando la desaparición forzada ya es reconocida como un crimen de lesa humanidad.",
-          "english": "Countless cases remain unresolved, even though enforced disappearance is now recognized as a crime against humanity."
+          "spanish": "La Comisión Rettig afirmó que unas 2.300 personas murieron o desaparecieron.",
+          "english": "The Rettig Commission stated that about 2,300 people were killed or disappeared."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "Reach for *aun cuando* instead of aunque when the contrast you're describing is especially stark or surprising -- it carries more emphasis, closer to English even in the face of this than a plain even though."
+      "content": "Never put *que* or *a* after *según*: *según la CONADEP*, not *según que la CONADEP*. Two different figures are not a mistake: put each one next to its source and the reader can see why they differ."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-represionpolitica-04-cabe-destacar-que-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-represionpolitica-04-cabe-destacar-que-gr.json
index 90002bfe..96ae8379 100644
--- a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-represionpolitica-04-cabe-destacar-que-gr.json
+++ b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-represionpolitica-04-cabe-destacar-que-gr.json
@@ -1,35 +1,35 @@
 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.represionpolitica.04.cabe-destacar-que",
-  "title": "Cabe destacar que: señalar un dato importante",
+  "title": "The gerund: two actions at the same time",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "*Cabe destacar que* (it's worth noting that, notably) flags a fact the writer wants the reader to pay special attention to -- common in academic and historical writing to highlight something significant within a broader narrative. It's an impersonal construction: cabe doesn't change for person or number. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "content": "You know the gerund (*gerundio*) from B1 Unit 2: it shows actions that happen together. The gerund (*llevando*, *pidiendo*, *documentando*) says how or while the main action happened, and it has the same subject as the main verb. It is a good way to describe a protest: the people did one thing and, at the same time, another."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "Cabe destacar que, en 1980, Adolfo Pérez Esquivel recibió el Premio Nobel de la Paz por su trabajo en defensa de los derechos humanos.",
-          "english": "It's worth noting that, in 1980, Adolfo Pérez Esquivel received the Nobel Peace Prize for his human rights work."
+          "spanish": "Las Madres caminaban alrededor de la plaza llevando un pañuelo blanco.",
+          "english": "The Mothers walked around the square wearing a white headscarf."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Cabe destacar que buena parte de esta resistencia dependió también de redes internacionales de solidaridad.",
-          "english": "It's worth noting that much of this resistance also depended on international solidarity networks."
+          "spanish": "Pedían información sobre sus hijos marchando cada jueves.",
+          "english": "They asked for information about their children, marching every Thursday."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Cabe destacar que la Vicaría de la Solidaridad fue fundada directamente por la Iglesia católica, no por un partido político.",
-          "english": "It's worth noting that the Vicariate of Solidarity was founded directly by the Catholic Church, not by a political party."
+          "spanish": "La Vicaría ayudaba a las familias documentando los casos.",
+          "english": "The Vicaría helped the families by documenting the cases."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Cabe destacar que estos movimientos guerrilleros fueron, en general, minoritarios dentro del conjunto de la resistencia.",
-          "english": "It's worth noting that these guerrilla movements were, in general, a minority within the resistance as a whole."
+          "spanish": "Los exiliados hablaban de la represión publicando informes.",
+          "english": "The exiles spoke about the repression by publishing reports."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "*Cabe destacar que* is a fixed impersonal phrase, like hay que -- never conjugate destacar to agree with the fact being highlighted (never cabe destacan que, even with a plural subject afterward)."
+      "content": "The gerund never changes for gender or number: *llevando* for one person or for many. It is not a second finite verb: *marchaban pidiendo*, not *marchaban pidieron*."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-represionpolitica-05-a-dia-de-hoy-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-represionpolitica-05-a-dia-de-hoy-gr.json
index b8c30601..9c93446b 100644
--- a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-represionpolitica-05-a-dia-de-hoy-gr.json
+++ b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-represionpolitica-05-a-dia-de-hoy-gr.json
@@ -1,35 +1,35 @@
 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.represionpolitica.05.a-dia-de-hoy",
-  "title": "A día de hoy: hablar de la situación actual",
+  "title": "Imperfect and present perfect: how things have changed",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "*A día de hoy* (as of today, to this day) anchors a statement firmly in the present, often to contrast it with an earlier historical situation -- useful for closing a historical narrative by noting what still holds true, or what has changed, up to now. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "content": "You know the contrast between the *imperfecto* (imperfect) and the *pretérito perfecto* (present perfect) for change over time from B1 Unit 5. The imperfect describes how things used to be (*antes, las víctimas no hablaban*). The present perfect describes what has changed up to now (*hoy muchas han hablado*). Memory and justice are about exactly this: what was true under the dictatorships and what has happened since."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "A día de hoy, tras la anulación de estas leyes de amnistía, cientos de nuevos juicios se han reabierto en Argentina.",
-          "english": "As of today, following the annulment of these amnesty laws, hundreds of new trials have reopened in Argentina."
+          "spanish": "Antes, muchas víctimas no hablaban de lo que pasaba.",
+          "english": "Before, many victims did not talk about what was happening."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "En Chile, la ley de amnistía de 1978 sigue generando, a día de hoy, un intenso debate legal y político.",
-          "english": "In Chile, the 1978 amnesty law is still generating, to this day, intense legal and political debate."
+          "spanish": "Con los años, muchas han dado su testimonio.",
+          "english": "Over the years, many have given their testimony."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "A día de hoy, la tensión entre memoria, justicia y reconciliación sigue sin resolverse completamente.",
-          "english": "As of today, the tension between memory, justice and reconciliation still hasn't been fully resolved."
+          "spanish": "Antes, los jefes militares no eran juzgados; desde 2003 se han reabierto muchos juicios.",
+          "english": "Before, military leaders were not tried; since 2003 many trials have been reopened."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "A día de hoy, ese antiguo centro de detención es un sitio de memoria abierto al público.",
-          "english": "As of today, that former detention center is a memory site open to the public."
+          "spanish": "Antes, el pasado era un tema prohibido; hoy se ha convertido en un tema de debate.",
+          "english": "Before, the past was a forbidden subject; today it has become a subject of debate."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "*A día de hoy* usually pairs with the present perfect (se han reabierto) or the simple present (sigue generando) -- it's about what's still true now, so it rarely appears with a past tense on its own."
+      "content": "Time words help you choose. *Antes* and *durante la dictadura* point to the imperfect. *Hoy*, *desde 2003* and *con los años* point to the present perfect, because the change still matters now."
     }
   ]
 }
